cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite the paper below."
title: "Precise but Uncoupled: Reviewer Precision Does Not Guarantee Critique Uptake in Multi-Agent Math Reasoning"
abstract: >-
  Many recent math- and science-oriented agent systems adopt hierarchical designs
  with specialized reviewer roles, assuming that a dedicated review stage helps
  turn wrong candidates into correct ones. We test that assumption on 4,181
  verifier-grounded Omni-MATH problems using matched gpt-oss-120b actors. From
  tier 4 onward collaboration gains open sharply, and in that harder regime
  broadcast-style peer discussion reaches higher final accuracy than a
  planner-executor-reviewer pipeline (PER). The gap is not explained by reviewer
  precision alone: PER's reviewer is more precise (0.861 vs 0.644), yet verified
  useful critique is much less likely to change the next candidate and produces
  lower reviewer-guided repair. Reviewer detection quality and critique uptake
  are empirically separable.
